### Actions become tokens {#action-token-intuition}

Each step from the transformer to the LLM, the VLM and native multimodal models followed the same play: find a way to tokenize a new modality, feed it to the same architecture, scale. The robotics version adds one more modality, actions, and treats control as **multimodal sequence prediction**: language tokens, image tokens, then action tokens that drive the motors (@fig-vla) [▶ 39:22](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2362). Attention over the whole history also answers the memory problem from the start of the lecture.

Actions are continuous, so the first VLAs (RT-2, OpenVLA) bin them, one dimension at a time and one time step at a time [▶ 40:22](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2422). Each dimension is normalized to $[-1, 1]$ using its 1st and 99th percentiles over the training data, clipped, and cut into 256 equal bins [▶ 40:46](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2446). RT-2 first used the minimum and maximum instead, but a single outlier then stretches the range: in this page's action toy, one glitched reading among 30\,000 makes min-max normalization use only 48 of the 256 bins for the real motions, against all 256 with the quantile bounds. The 256 bins become 256 tokens of the language model's vocabulary, for example by overwriting its 256 least-used tokens [▶ 42:10](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2530), and the model is trained by next-token prediction with its usual loss. [Week 1](w01-introduction.qmd#tokens) tokenized a motion this way.

