**What to watch for.** All three panels show the same run (seed 0), drawn together behind one cursor so the schemes can be compared step by step. In the top panel every step follows a new noisy estimate of the goal, so the orange line is slightly ragged all the way. In the middle panel a faint chunk appears every half second, and the line follows each one smoothly. Where one chunk hands over to the next and a new estimate takes over, it shows a small kink: the typical bend there is 18 times the typical one inside a chunk. When the goal moves, at 1\.6 s, the orange line turns at once. The blue line stays level, carrying on with its old chunk until the next query at 2\.0 s, and it ends a little off the new goal, since its last chunk trusted one estimate. In the bottom panel a chunk appears at *every* step, so a fan of overlapping predictions runs ahead of the line. The line is their average: as smooth as the blue one, and closer to the goal. After the move, the newest predictions bend down at once, and the green line starts down with them, but more gently than the orange one, because old predictions still hold it back. A last small kink near 2\.1 s marks the step after which no chunk made before the move is left in the average.

### Why bins break at high control rates {#fast-intuition}

Chunks of 1,400 numbers were fine for ACT, which outputs them all at once. An autoregressive VLA must generate them one token at a time, and per-step binning breaks down as the control rate rises [▶ 47:06](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2826). At high rates consecutive actions barely differ [▶ 47:21](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2841). Next-token prediction learns from what each new token adds, and when tokens are this correlated, a model can get a low loss by copying the previous action token, without learning the skill [▶ 47:53](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2873).

The action toy measures this on smooth one-second motions in 2 dimensions, each a sum of 3 slow waves (up to 1 Hz). Binned at 5 Hz, "repeat the previous token" predicts 45% of a token's variation; at 50 Hz it predicts 99\.4% (@fig-tokens, right). Exact repeats remain rare, 11% at 50 Hz, since 256 bins resolve small changes: the token is not literally the same, it is just almost entirely predictable. Meanwhile the sequence gets long: 100 tokens for each second of motion.

The fix is to compress first [▶ 48:26](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2906): find units for motion larger than one time step and smaller than a whole trajectory, as sub-words are for text and patches for images. BPE alone suits discrete symbols, not continuous signals, and a learned vector quantizer adds another model to train. **FAST** borrows from JPEG instead [▶ 49:43](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=2983). A smooth signal has nearly all its energy at low frequencies, so a **discrete cosine transform** of each action dimension leaves a few large coefficients and many near zero. Scale and round them, list them lowest frequency first, and run BPE on the resulting integers, which merges the runs of zeros and common patterns into a few dense tokens. In the toy at 50 Hz, a chunk becomes 10\.7 FAST tokens on average instead of 100 bins, 9 times fewer, and reconstructs the motion with about the same error (0\.049 against 0\.044 for the bins, on motions with standard deviation 0\.79). At 2 Hz there is little to compress: 2\.7 tokens against 4, and a larger error than the bins.

In the paper's experiments, with a VLA trained on FAST tokens, performance keeps improving at higher control rates where binning collapses [▶ 50:59](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=3059). An autoregressive version of π0 trained on FAST tokens reached the performance of the flow-matching π0 (the [Week 6](w06-generative-models.qmd) kind of action head) five times faster in training [▶ 51:56](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=3116), followed language commands zero-shot in unseen kitchens after training on the DROID data set [▶ 52:13](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=3133), and folded laundry at 50 Hz [▶ 52:36](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=3156). (The lecturer is a co-author of FAST.)

The lecture's summary [▶ 53:24](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=3204): treat robotics as sequence modeling, build on transformers because they scale, find the right tokens for each modality, and let data and compute do the rest, which is the bitter lesson applied to robots [▶ 54:54](https://youtu.be/imSTfMJjp7M?t=3294).

